\title{Transformer Encoder Layer}
\description{
A single transformer encoder layer as described in \emph{Attention Is All You Need}, consisting of a
multi-head self-attention block and a position-wise feed-forward network, each wrapped in a
residual connection and layer normalization.

This is a thin wrapper around \code{\link[torch:nn_transformer_encoder_layer]{torch::nn_transformer_encoder_layer()}} that makes it usable as a
building block of a \code{\link[mlr3pipelines:Graph]{Graph}} of tensor operations.
}
\section{Tensor Layout}{

Input and output are \verb{(batch, sequence, feature)}, i.e. the \code{batch_first} layout of
\code{\link[torch:nn_transformer_encoder_layer]{torch::nn_transformer_encoder_layer()}}, which is fixed and not a hyperparameter.
\code{torch} defaults to \verb{(sequence, batch, feature)}, but the first dimension of every shape has to
be the batch dimension here.

The layer preserves the shape of its input, so the output shape is the input shape.
Only the feature dimension has to be known, the sequence length may be \code{NA}.
}

\section{nn_module}{

Calls \code{\link[torch:nn_transformer_encoder_layer]{torch::nn_transformer_encoder_layer()}} when trained, where the parameter \code{d_model} is
inferred as the last dimension of the input tensor and \code{batch_first} is always \code{TRUE}, see
section \emph{Tensor Layout}.
}

\section{Parameters}{

\itemize{
\item \code{nhead} :: \code{integer(1)}\cr
Number of parallel attention heads. The feature dimension must be divisible by \code{nhead}.
\item \code{dim_feedforward} :: \code{integer(1)}\cr
The hidden dimension of the feed-forward network.
Default is \code{2048}.
\item \code{dropout} :: \code{numeric(1)}\cr
Dropout probability, applied to both the attention weights and the feed-forward network.
Default is \code{0.1}.
\item \code{activation} :: \code{character(1)} | \code{function}\cr
The activation function of the feed-forward network, either \code{"relu"}, \code{"gelu"}, or a function
mapping a \code{\link[torch:torch_tensor]{torch_tensor}} to a \code{\link[torch:torch_tensor]{torch_tensor}}.
Default is \code{"relu"}.
\item \code{layer_norm_eps} :: \code{numeric(1)}\cr
A value added to the denominator of the layer normalizations for numerical stability.
Default is \code{1e-5}.
\item \code{norm_first} :: \code{logical(1)}\cr
Whether to apply layer normalization before the attention and feed-forward sublayers
(pre-norm) instead of after them (post-norm).
Default is \code{FALSE}.
\item \code{bias} :: \code{logical(1)}\cr
Whether the linear layers use a bias and the layer normalizations learn affine parameters.
Default is \code{TRUE}.
\item \code{is_causal} :: \code{logical(1)}\cr
Whether to apply a causal mask, i.e. whether a position may only attend to the positions up to
and including itself. This cannot be combined with the \code{"src_mask"} input channel.
Default is \code{FALSE}.
}

Note that \code{d_model} is \emph{not} a parameter, as it is inferred from the shape of the input tensor,
and that \code{batch_first} is \emph{not} a parameter either, as it is fixed to \code{TRUE}, see section
\emph{Tensor Layout}.
}

\section{Input and Output Channels}{

There is always one input channel \code{"input"}, which is the sequence the layer attends over, and
one output channel \code{"output"} of the same shape.

The construction arguments \code{src_mask} and \code{src_key_padding_mask} each add a further input
channel of that name, which is passed to the corresponding argument of the module's \code{forward()}
method. They are construction arguments and not hyperparameters, because they determine the
structure of the \code{\link[mlr3pipelines:Graph]{Graph}}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"src_mask"}: an additive or boolean mask over the sequence, of shape \verb{(sequence, sequence)}
or \verb{(batch * nhead, sequence, sequence)}. Positions that are \code{TRUE} are \emph{not} attended to.
\item \code{"src_key_padding_mask"}: a mask of shape \verb{(batch, sequence)} marking the padding positions of
each observation, which are \code{TRUE}.
}

Note that a \verb{(sequence, sequence)} \code{"src_mask"} has no batch dimension, unlike every other tensor
in a network, so its first dimension is the sequence length rather than the batch size.

For an explanation see \code{\link{PipeOpTorch}}.
}

